NNAAMMEE
     veldiff - what changed between two revisions of a drawing

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     vveellddiiffff _o_l_d._d _n_e_w._d
     vveellddiiffff -mmaarrkk _o_l_d._d _n_e_w._d > _m_a_r_k_u_p._d

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     vveellddiiffff compares two revisions of a drawing made with
     vveelllluumm.

     Matching is by IDENTITY, not by line.  ddiiffff(1) on two .dd
     files drowns in reordering, because the line order is the Z-
     ORDER and not the content.  Here symbols match by DESIGNATOR
     first - R7 is R7 wherever it moved in the list - and every-
     thing else by exact (type, geometry, value) equality.  There
     is no fuzzy matching and no tolerance: an object that moved
     one unit is a remove plus an add, and on a grid that is the
     honest answer.  An object still standing exactly where it
     stood, saying something else, reports as CHANGED.

     One line each, in the vveellcchheecckk shape:

                  new.d: R7 moved 40,20 -> 40,28
                  new.d: R9 added (R 47k)
                  new.d: C2 removed (C 100n)
                  new.d: T "Supply, sheet 1" changed

     The EXIT STATUS is the change count (capped at 254), so a
     Makefile gates a release print on ``no drift since the ap-
     proved revision'' exactly the way it gates on vveellcchheecckk. A
     change of sheet UNITS is reported rather than refused.

TTHHEE MMAARRKKUUPP
     -mmaarrkk prints the answer AS A DRAWING on standard output:
     everything unchanged on its own layers, ADDED objects bold,
     REMOVED objects dashed on the annotation layer.  There is no
     new viewer and no new user interface, because the markup is
     ORDINARY OBJECTS - vveellppiicc draws it, vveellpplloott prints it, and
     the editor opens it.

RREEVVIISSIIOONNSS
     A revision is a FILE, and the record is the directory: no
     SCCS, no hidden state, no format change.  When the frame
     stamp's $RR says B, ccpp aammpp11.dd aammpp11.dd.BB is the whole ceremony.

     The /usr/vellum/etc/project.mk skeleton turns that into a
     workflow: rreevv: copies every sheet to its ._R_E_V twin, ddrriifftt:
     runs this program on each sheet against that twin so its
     exit status gates pprriinntt:, and rreevviieeww: hangs the marked-up
     drawing on the wall instead.  The gallery ships the pair:
     /usr/vellum/eg/amp.d.A beside amp.d.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
                  veldiff amp.d.A amp.d


